{
  "abstract": "We study whether the classical modular-hyperbola construction for the no-three-in-line problem can be improved by changing its integer lifts or repairing its core with additional reciprocal conics. Our principal result is a uniform secant-saturation theorem. In the square of side $2p$, retain the classical $2(p-1)$-point arm core for a nonzero multiplier $c$. For every $\\varepsilon>0$, the number of lifts of any distinct nonzero multiplier $d$ compatible with the core after deleting a subset $D$ is at most $\\varepsilon p+C_\\varepsilon|D|$, for all sufficiently large primes, uniformly in both multipliers. Consequently, a fixed finite palette of reciprocal conics cannot improve the asymptotic coefficient $3/2$ while deleting only $o(p)$ core points. The proof retains shared integer congruence phases in a finite-direction curve law, evaluates a blocking mean exactly, and uses two finite slope families to obtain uniformity.\n\nA secondary comparison transfers Prellberg's published continuum upper coefficient $1.5768233968738\\ldots$ uniformly as $p$ grows. For each fixed lift factor, we prove that the optimum divided by $p$ converges uniformly to a computable Lipschitz function of the translation. Finite local policies approximate that limit and yield certificates for strict limiting density gains, if such gains exist. A standalone supplement regenerates exact finite arithmetic and geometric checks; the general policy compiler is not implemented. These results neither improve the unrestricted asymptotic lower bound nor resolve the target construction problem.",
